{% comment %}
  Waitlist form — restores the block that clippargolf.com renders inside the
  hero, and that the initial port dropped because it POSTed to /api/submit on
  Vercel (no equivalent on a storefront).

  Capture path is now Shopify-native: `{% form 'customer' %}` creates a customer
  with email-marketing consent and posts to /contact. Nothing here holds a
  Sender API token — Sender's Shopify app syncs those customers into the
  waitlist group, so the secret stays server-side where it belongs.

  Markup, IDs and copy are kept identical to the source page so assets/clippar.css
  applies unchanged and assets/clippar.js can bind to the same element IDs.

  Frequency rides along as a customer tag (`plays-weekly` etc.) because the
  customer form has no arbitrary-field storage. Tag writes via contact[tags]
  need confirming against the live store — see MIGRATION.md.

  Accepts:
    heading, body, note — copy overrides. All optional.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign heading = heading | default: 'Join the waitlist'
  assign body = body | default: 'Get founding member pricing and be first to know when we launch.'
  assign note = note | default: 'No spam. Unsubscribe anytime. Founding member pricing locked in for early signups.'
-%}

<div class="form-wrap" id="waitlist-form-section">
  <div class="form-card">
    <h2>{{ heading }}</h2>
    <p>{{ body }}</p>
    <div class="form-error" id="form-error"></div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Success and error states live INSIDE the form block: `form.posted_successfully?`
      and `form.errors` are only in scope between {% form %} and {% endform %}.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- form 'customer', id: 'waitlist-form', class: 'waitlist-form' -%}
      {%- if form.posted_successfully? -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Post-submit state. This is not a thank-you line any more — it is the
          beta invitation, and it is the only place it appears (no new page, no
          email). Henry's reasoning, so nobody "improves" it later: the risk
          with beta testers is a bad first impression, and the fix for that is
          setting expectations, not hiding rough edges. A tester who was warned
          forgives a crash; one who was not leaves a one-star review that sits
          on a brand-new App Store listing forever. **Do not trim, hedge or
          soften the "what to be aware of" list.**
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="success-state beta-invite" id="success" style="display:block;">
          <div class="tick">
            <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#a8e63d" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3>You're on the list.</h3>

          <p class="beta-lede">Want to try the app while you wait?</p>

          {%- comment -%}
            Three lines, big, no preamble. Henry rewrote this himself on the
            night of 25 Aug after seeing the longer two-column version: he
            wanted it simple enough to read off a phone in a few seconds, with
            the "it's a beta, expect bugs" warning first rather than buried
            under a pitch. Keep the warning in line one, and keep it to three.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <ul class="beta-points">
            <li>This is V1 beta. Expect bugs and UI issues — we're fixing them fast.</li>
            <li>We just want feedback right now. There aren't many of us, so yours changes what gets built.</li>
            <li>Testers get a free kit to try on course, before anyone can buy one.</li>
          </ul>

          {%- comment -%}
            A real anchor, not a button with a JS handler. On iOS an
            apps.apple.com link hands straight to the App Store app, and it
            still works with JS off or if the tag POST below fails. The click
            handler in clippar.js piggybacks the tagging on top; it never
            blocks the navigation.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <a class="btn-submit beta-cta"
             id="beta-cta"
             href="https://apps.apple.com/au/app/clippar-golf-highlights/id6788301452?ct=sd26-waitlist&amp;mt=8"
             target="_blank"
             rel="noopener">Try the beta on the App Store</a>
        </div>
      {%- else -%}
        {%- if form.errors -%}
          <div class="form-error" style="display:block;">
            {{ form.errors.messages['form'] | default: 'Something went wrong. Please try again.' }}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          Marks this submission as a waitlist signup rather than a checkout-created
          customer, and carries the play-frequency answer. clippar.js rewrites the
          tag list before the native submit; without JS the customer still lands
          tagged, just without the frequency.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <input type="hidden" name="contact[tags]" id="f-tags" value="waitlist">

        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="f-name">Your name</label>
          <input type="text" id="f-name" name="contact[first_name]" placeholder="Tiger" required autocomplete="name">
        </div>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="f-email">Email address</label>
          <input type="email" id="f-email" name="contact[email]" placeholder="you@gmail.com" required autocomplete="email">
        </div>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="f-frequency">How often do you play?</label>
          {%- comment -%}
            `name` matters more than it looks. Without it the browser never puts
            this field in the submission, so every answer was being thrown away
            — while `required` still forced each signup to pick one. All of the
            friction, none of the data. The naming follows the contact form's
            `contact[Enquiry type]` convention so it lands as a labelled
            attribute on the Shopify customer.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <select id="f-frequency" name="contact[Play frequency]" required>
            <option value="" disabled selected>Select frequency</option>
            <option value="weekly">Every week</option>
            <option value="fortnightly">Fortnightly</option>
            <option value="monthly">Once a month</option>
            <option value="occasional">Occasional</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <button class="btn-submit" type="submit" id="btn-submit">SECURE MY SPOT</button>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endform -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      "Who clicked through to the beta", not "how many clicks".

      The only way to write to a customer record from the storefront is the
      customer form, and Shopify's spam protection (see the bootstrap in every
      page's <head>) is wired to a **submit event listener on document**. So:

        * `form.submit()` fires no submit event -> no hCaptcha token ->
          `400 Missing CAPTCHA token`. Same trap as gotcha #1 in the shopify
          brief, just reached from the other direction.
        * `form.requestSubmit()` does fire one, so the token gets attached.
        * `data-shopify-captcha="true"` makes the bootstrap bind this form at
          DOMContentLoaded instead of at click time, so the token is already
          sitting in the form when the visitor taps through.

      It posts into a hidden iframe so the tab stays on the success panel and
      the App Store link is free to open. clippar.js fills in the email (from
      `form.email` if Shopify hands it back, otherwise from what the visitor
      typed a moment ago, kept in sessionStorage) and re-sends the whole tag
      list, so the tagging is correct whether Shopify merges tags or replaces
      them. If there is no email to attribute the click to it does not fire at
      all — a counter that cannot name a person is not what was asked for.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <iframe name="beta-tag-sink" id="beta-tag-sink" title="" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"
            style="display:none;width:0;height:0;border:0;"></iframe>
    {%- form 'customer', id: 'beta-tag-form', class: 'beta-tag-form', target: 'beta-tag-sink', data-shopify-captcha: 'true' -%}
      <input type="hidden" name="contact[email]" id="beta-tag-email" value="{{ form.email }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="contact[tags]" id="beta-tag-tags" value="waitlist, beta-clicked">
    {%- endform -%}

    <p class="form-note">{{ note }}</p>
  </div>
</div>
